rkable, and tonally clear."}, {"id": "5d140625-8355-40d3-9cee-09c53066793e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Answer and Entries", "children": [{"id": "4ff521ab-5baf-4d96-89ae-7ed5f1544ec3", "type": "concept", "title": "Dominant answer"}, {"id": "bcb8b041-827b-40bb-8e7e-7904738701d4", "type": "concept", "title": "Tonal adjustment"}, {"id": "ec9dbe95-a103-45fe-a058-723339fc0b26", "type": "concept", "title": "Order of entries and voice range"}], "description": "Students distinguish real and tonal answer decisions."}, {"id": "304f5200-fe9d-442b-99e9-0ed54bfbc4b6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Countersubject and Free Counterpoint", "children": [{"id": "ed91fe5d-37e3-4399-8d16-f460d165bb9a", "type": "concept", "title": "Invertible countersubject"}, {"id": "fd041877-6ccd-49da-ad6e-fcdb8dcf3c35", "type": "concept", "title": "Free counterpoint"}, {"id": "3b6bfccd-0d4b-4923-8718-d232baba160d", "type": "concept", "title": "Texture after entries"}], "description": "Accompanying material must support future development."}]}, {"id": "0ef92b39-566c-4af5-8205-f067b984702e", "type": "topic", "title": "Fugal Development", "description": "Development makes fugue more than a procession of entries. Students study episode, modulation, stretto, pedal, augmentation, diminution, and formal pacing.", "children": [{"id": "ae03d6d6-ee73-4ffa-a636-56e3f296e83c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Episodes and Modulation", "children": [{"id": "569c32b4-d65c-414a-a68d-fd819cbce060", "type": "concept", "title": "Sequential episodes"}, {"id": "faae4728-e534-4614-ad75-fb1c7695b346", "type": "concept", "title": "Circle-of-fifths motion"}, {"id": "2f9cef51-ccdf-4199-a4e9-0d980d52837e", "type": "concept", "title": "Fragmentation and recombination"}], "description": "Students learn to travel between entries with motivic continuity."}, {"id": "f862ee13-3c87-4c45-a797-89f784158d26", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intensification Techniques", "children": [{"id": "4a3cacaa-eb9a-4902-8f8e-ba7550c88b9d", "type": "co